      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_CONCEPTS_01",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__6202B09B_1",
          "title": "1. Key Academic Principles: Circular Dynamics & Road Banking",
          "contentMarkdown": "Centripetal acceleration in uniform circular motion is directed radially inward with magnitude a_c = v^2 / R. Centripetal force F_c = (m * v^2) / R is the net inward radial force required to maintain circular motion. On a flat level circular road, the maximum speed without skidding is v_max = sqrt(mu_s * R * g). On a frictionless banked circular road, the optimum safe speed is v_optimum = sqrt(R * g * tan(theta)).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_49F23753",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_707F0025",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_99AAE05A",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_EC2AF8F9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_CONCEPTS_02",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__6202B09B_2",
          "title": "2. Key Academic Principles: Circular Dynamics & Road Banking",
          "contentMarkdown": "On a banked road with friction, the maximum safe speed is v_max = sqrt(R * g * ((mu_s + tan(theta)) / (1 - mu_s * tan(theta)))). Banking of roads directs the horizontal component of the normal reaction inward to provide centripetal force. When a particle moves in a circular path of radius R with constant speed v, it experiences continuous acceleration directed toward the center of the circle called centripetal acceleration: a_c = v^2 / R = omega^2 * R. Centripetal force is not a new fundamental force, but is provided by real physical forces (e.g., tension in string, gravitational attraction, electrostatic force, or friction).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_22CDC09D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_4B10DF30",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_575CE100",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_AA0228B3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_CONCEPTS_03",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__6202B09B_3",
          "title": "3. Key Academic Principles: Circular Dynamics & Road Banking",
          "contentMarkdown": "Motion of a Car on a Level Circular Road: The centripetal force is provided entirely by static friction between the tires and the road surface: (m * v^2) / R <= mu_s * m * g. Motion of a Car on a Banked Circular Road: To reduce reliance on friction and prevent skidding, roads are banked at an angle theta with the horizontal.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_CA151C5D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_5C43B4D3",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_942649B0",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_9EF5E8F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_CONCEPTS_04",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR__6202B09B_4",
          "title": "4. Key Academic Principles: Circular Dynamics & Road Banking",
          "contentMarkdown": "When friction is present, the maximum permissible speed on a banked road without skidding up the incline is: On a frictionless banked road, the horizontal component of the normal reaction provides the necessary centripetal force (N * sin(theta) = m * v^2 / R, and N * cos(theta) = m * g).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_7BB1A4C6",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_6202B09B_84C04745"
          ]
        }
      ],
